thank you very much Tony thank you to  our panel I frankly am looking forward  to hearing their remarks today and so  mine are going to be very brief but  thank you to everyone in the audience  this is really a remarkable crowd an  overflow room is always a great thing to  see especially at a time of the year  when people have so much going on with  this place going back to school with  kids back in the classroom this is  really an amazing turnout and frankly a  testament to how seriously we take this  issue here in Connecticut I've chosen to  refocus and reup my work on the issue of  climate change as a new United States  Senator for one simple reason during the  very short time that I have been in  federal public office this now being my  seventh year in those very short seven  years what began as the worst-case  estimates about sea level rise and  temperature rise over the rest of this  century are now the mainstream  scientific estimates in only a half a  decade all of a sudden we have come to a  consensus that some of our worst fears  now may be coming true during that exact  same time that I've been in the house  and in the Senate I've become a father I  have a five-year-old and a one-year-old  at home and I shudder to think about  what this world is going to look like at  the end of this century when I hope that  they may still be living on this earth  if we don't make changes today because  this is one of those issues that if you  don't change habits now the trend lines  may be so locked in that they cannot be  reversed by my children no matter how  well I raise them no matter how smart  they are how prescient they become no  matter how turned on their generation is  to this issue it may be too late and so  that's why so many of us who came to the  house of the Senate maybe five or ten  years ago caring about this issue now  have it at the top of our priority list  because we see a very short time window  in terms of our ability to act and the  reason why we focused here on  connecticut is because just in the last  12 months we have had first  witness to the devastation that climate  change can bring to this state we  probably all remember where we were the  night that storm sandy hit I was at home  in Cheshire our power had gone out we  didn't have a backup generator I had a  little bit of power left on my phone and  I was sitting up with our one lamp in  our bedroom at about the witching hour  of eleven or twelve o'clock locked onto  the small battery power that was sending  me tweets from norwalk and Stanford not  knowing whether or not the world was  ending in those cities not understanding  whether those places would still be  there the next morning we barely  survived sandy and yet the devastation  still totals across the stretch and the  billions we've seen what it can do here  in Connecticut and we know how  vulnerable we are as the panelists will  tell you we are really unique we are a  coastline state where almost all of our  economic assets essentially run right  along the stretch of the coastline the  two major thoroughfares of 95 and the  Amtrak line every major economic hub  save for Hartford along the coastline  some of our biggest economic drivers  like the casinos for instance within a  relative short distance from the coast  storms that used to be happening every  12 months in Connecticut are happening  every six months now just in 10 years  sea levels rising by an inch per decade  for Connecticut's standpoint we don't  survive as a state or an economy if we  don't get this right in the short run  and so that's why we wanted to convene  this group here today to expand upon a  conversation that's happening at  multiple levels so excited to hear about  the announcement that you just heard  about a new climate prediction tool  specifically dedicated to the Northeast  that Yale and the center are going to be  investing in that is something that is  badly badly needed because we are no  longer as interested in simply  addicting how big the next storm is  going to be we need in order to do real  land use planning for instance in this  state amid shrinking resources to know  what's happening in the five and  ten-year time window that's what's  happening here in Connecticut and this  is a focus here on the Northeast but  given the fact that this will be  probably the only two hour time block in  this week last week and the next week  that I won't be thinking about Syria let  me make this contextual for you for a  second because this is about the effects  on the Northeast but from 2006 to 2011  rainfall in Syria fell to below eight  inches per year which is the absolute  minimum level necessary to be able to  kick-out abril agriculture going without  irrigation during that time in order to  make do with less water tens of  thousands of new wells were drilled all  throughout Syria having the effect of  dropping the water table below the level  that any of the pumps could reach  agriculture during that five year period  of time disappeared in many regions of  Syria and in the regions in which it  remained was reduced by about 75 to 85  percent over that time stretch two to  three million of Syria's 10 million  people fell into destitute poverty  bumping up against all of the political  refugees over the last ten years that it  spilled in from Israel and Iraq and  other places there are a lot of reasons  why we're talking about Syria today it's  not just about a massive drought that  was made much worse by temperatures  already increased in Syria and the  region that created a vicious cycle of  evaporation that made this drought worse  than any of the other droughts that it  hit there's a lot of other reasons at  the top of the list still being the  brutal repression of Assad and his  family on the people of that country but  make no mistake the turbulence that we  have seen in Syria and the Middle East  is a consequence of climate change  consequence of droughts that when they  happen today happen harder tougher with  more dire consequences than they ever  did before we are seeing the results of  climate change in the Middle East and  while we may never become that fractious  here we know that if we don't make some  changes here in Connecticut dire times  in a different fashion await us as well  last thing to say is about politics I  don't want to talk too much about  partisan politics here today except to  say that the effort on climate  communication is critical and still at  the top of the list as far as I'm  concerned right now in Washington about  fifty six percent of the House  Republican caucus denies the climate  change exists about sixty percent of the  Senate Republican caucus one hundred  percent of the Republicans that sit on  the Environment Committee in the Senate  our climate deniers as well as 17 of 22  that said in the Science Committee in 22  of 30 that's it on the Energy Committee  if we don't invest in education about  the 99 percent of scientists who tell us  that our actions are contributing to  global warming we can't win this fight  in Washington we can't get anything done  we have to engage in a robust battle to  change public opinion to make sure that  people are sent to Washington that  believe in science again or all of the  work that we are doing on mitigation and  new plans to reduce carbon emissions  aren't going to mean anything that being  said there is some really good news  happening around the globe buried amidst  the talk of Syria at the g20 was a  breakthrough on the issue of hfcs which  are the substitute for the stuff that we  got rid of in the montreal protocols  that was burning a hole through the  ozone layer hfcs the substitute for CFCs  are now one of the most insidious  Climate Pollutants in that they get into  the atmosphere and trap heat at a rate  frankly that is much worse than  traditional carbon dioxide and for the  first time China at the g20  made a commitment to begin a contact  group with the United States to come up  with some new standards to reduce the  usage of hfcs and the other g20 nations  including developing nations like Brazil  and India agreed to a slightly more  beginning step at the same work as well  that's really really good news nations  that we had just a devil of a time  getting to the table to get serious  about this issue now realize that it is  in their interest to be with us today on  Climate Pollutants maybe carbon dioxide  is a tougher nut to crack but if you can  take on some of these short-lived  fast-acting Climate Pollutants things  like hfcs or methane or black carbon we  can get a lot of pretty important work  done so as bad as things may seem in  Washington there are so there is some  good news when it comes to the  international climate change front and  it matters to everybody who lives on  this world but here in Connecticut as  you will hear it likely matters as much  if not more than everywhere else we've  got a fantastic panel here today I'm  eager to hear your questions and your  thoughts as well thank you to the Yale  climate and energy institute for hosting  us here today and I hope we have a great  discussion thank you very much everyone
 

